(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-cp-exam-1-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"Clin Path I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form B","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What proportion of severe blindness from macular degeneration does the wet form account for?","choices":["About fifty per cent","About twenty-five per cent","About ten per cent","About ninety per cent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — despite the dry form being commoner. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Over what timescale does acute inflammation occur, and when does it begin?","choices":["Over minutes to hours only, beginning after a full 24 hours have elapsed since the injury","Over seconds, minutes, hours or days, beginning within seconds of tissue injury","Over days to months, beginning once macrophages arrive","Over weeks, beginning after antibody production starts"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute inflammation begins immediately — within seconds — following injury of tissues. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What protects the outer third of the ear canal?","choices":["Ciliated epithelium sweeping debris outward","A keratin plug at the isthmus","Cerumen from ceruminous glands, and hair follicles","Mucus from goblet cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both sit in the outer third, which is why impaction and otitis externa are canal diseases. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which cell types characterise chronic inflammation on review?","choices":["Basophils and mast cells only","Fibroblasts in the absence of leukocytes","Neutrophils and eosinophils predominantly, without mononuclear infiltrate","Macrophages, monocytes and mononuclear cells including lymphocytes and plasma cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The review slide lists exactly these, accompanied by proliferation of blood vessels and connective tissue. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"How do eccrine and apocrine sweat glands differ in secretion and function?","choices":["Eccrine secrete keratin and apocrine secrete melanin","Eccrine open onto the skin surface and secrete water and electrolytes for cooling by evaporation; apocrine sit mainly in the axilla and anogenital areas and secrete protein and fatty lipids, functioning as scent glands","Eccrine glands sit in the axilla and anogenital areas and secrete protein and fatty lipids; apocrine glands open onto the surface of the skin and secrete water and electrolytes for cooling the body","Both secrete sebum, differing only in the body regions they occupy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Body odour comes from bacterial degradation of apocrine sweat, not from the sweat itself. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which virus causes conjunctivitis?","choices":["Herpes simplex virus","Varicella zoster virus","Cytomegalovirus","Adenovirus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — adenovirus. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What defines telangiectasia at the tissue level?","choices":["Capillary rupture with blood deposited into the dermis","Dense inflammatory infiltration around dilated vessels","Proliferation of new vessels driven by release of vascular endothelial growth factor into the surrounding dermis over a prolonged period","Permanent dilatation and thinning of the endothelium of capillaries, venules and arterioles in the superficial dermis, with NO inflammatory cell infiltration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The absence of inflammatory infiltrate is the discriminating feature. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"In the reviewed sequence of acute inflammation, which vascular event comes FIRST?","choices":["Increased vascular permeability","Haemoconcentration and stasis","Vasoconstriction","Vasodilation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The sequence opens with vasoconstriction, followed by vasodilation, increased vascular permeability, then haemoconcentration and stasis. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What important caveat attaches to TNM definitions?","choices":["They replace histological grading, which is no longer used","They are cancer-specific; for some cancers depth of invasion matters more than size","They are identical across every cancer, which is what makes the system universal","They apply only to epithelial malignancies and not to sarcomas"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — which is why a staging table applies to one named cancer rather than all. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which three interneuron types modulate the retinal signal before it reaches the ganglion cells?","choices":["Horizontal, amacrine and Purkinje cells","Rod, cone and ganglion cells","Bipolar, horizontal and amacrine cells","Bipolar, Müller and astrocyte cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — those three make up the modulating network. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which cancer patients are described as sometimes carrying an inherited mutation of BRCA-1 or BRCA-2?","choices":["The majority of breast cancer patients","All patients with familial adenomatous polyposis","All patients with xeroderma pigmentosum","A minority of breast cancer patients"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a minority, not most. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What causes an organ to enlarge in hypertrophy, and where is it especially important?","choices":["An increase in the number of cells; especially important in labile tissues such as epithelium and bone marrow","Replacement of one mature cell type by another; especially important at epithelial junctions","Autonomous proliferation of a clone of cells; especially important in glandular tissue","Enlargement of the individual cells; especially important in permanent tissues such as skeletal and cardiac muscle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it may also occur in other tissues as part of adaptive growth. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is a fissure, and what conditions produce one?","choices":["A linear ulcer forming a crack in the epidermis, from loss of elasticity or flexibility, severe dryness and mechanical tension, with hyperkeratosis thickening the stratum corneum","A superficial loss of the epidermis that spares the dermal-epidermal junction and everything beneath it, so no collagen is ever destroyed in the process of healing","A thickened plaque produced by chronic rubbing","A dilated superficial vessel without inflammatory infiltrate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is a linear form of ulceration rather than a distinct process. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is the classic symptom tetrad of Meniere disease?","choices":["Continuous vertigo lasting days, unilateral tinnitus, unilateral loss, and nystagmus","Vertigo lasting under a minute on head movement, with no hearing loss","High-frequency hearing loss with tinnitus and no vertigo","Episodic vertigo for hours, low-frequency tinnitus, low-tone loss, aural fullness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All four, and the LOW-frequency emphasis is what distinguishes the hearing loss from presbycusis or noise damage, which take the high frequencies first. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which pairing of endogenous and exogenous toxic agents in chronic inflammation is correct?","choices":["Endogenous — atherosclerosis; exogenous — particulates such as silica","Endogenous — bacterial infection; exogenous — viral infection","Endogenous — silica; exogenous — atherosclerosis","Endogenous — rheumatoid arthritis; exogenous — lupus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atherosclerosis is the endogenous example and particulates such as silica the exogenous one. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the defect in conductive hearing loss, and where does it sit?","choices":["Defective sound transmission, in the external or middle ear","Central processing failure in the auditory cortex","Destruction of hair cells or auditory nerve fibres, in the inner ear","Failure of glutamate release at cranial nerve VIII"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The apparatus that carries sound inward has failed; the cochlea and nerve are intact. That anatomical split is what the tuning fork tests are detecting. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Recognition and attachment during phagocytosis are enhanced by the presence of what?","choices":["Serum factors known as opsonins","Histamine released from basophils","Fibrinogen deposited by the coagulation system","Bradykinin generated from plasma proteins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The review names opsonins explicitly, with immunoglobulin G and complement fragment C3b as the examples. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"From how many cells is the proliferating population of a neoplasm derived?","choices":["One cell, which underwent a genetic alteration","Many cells, each of which underwent an independent genetic alteration","Two cells, one parenchymal and one stromal","A variable number, depending on the tissue in which it arises"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the whole population is clonal. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which receptors carry the sensations of touch and pressure?","choices":["Golgi tendon organs and muscle spindles","Langerhans cells and melanocytes","Meissner's corpuscles, Pacinian corpuscles and Merkel cells","Ruffini endings, Krause bulbs and free nerve endings only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Meissner's serve fine discriminative touch and low-frequency vibration, Pacinian respond to deep pressure and vibration, and Merkel cells serve light touch. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which signalling pathway does basal cell carcinoma overactivate, and in which cells?","choices":["Uninhibited melanocyte growth at the dermal-epidermal junction","The Hedgehog signalling pathway, following ultraviolet-induced mutation of basal keratinocytes in the epidermis","The tp53 tumour suppressor pathway, acting in the differentiated keratinocytes of the epidermis","Fibroblast growth factor receptor-3, in immature keratinocytes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is the most common skin cancer and the most common malignancy in humans, slow growing and rarely metastasising. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Why are nasal polyps described as non-neoplastic?","choices":["No new tissue is grown; existing mucosa becomes oedematous","They arise from cartilage rather than epithelium","They regress without treatment in every case","They are malignant but slow growing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. They are waterlogged mucous membrane rather than a growth. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why does psoriatic skin become dry?","choices":["The stratum corneum is lost entirely","Sebaceous glands overproduce sebum, which dries the surface","Epidermal cells fail to secrete lipids, producing xeroderma","Eccrine sweat glands are destroyed by inflammation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the dryness follows from a secretory failure rather than from water loss alone. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Roughly how many cones are there, and where are they concentrated?","choices":["About 120 million, concentrated in the peripheral retina rather than the fovea","About 6 million, spread evenly across the whole retina","About 60 million, concentrated at the optic disc","About 6 million, concentrated in the fovea centralis within the macula"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — that is the count and the location. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which leukocyte is named in the exudate of acute inflammation on the review slide?","choices":["The eosinophil","The lymphocyte","The neutrophil","The plasma cell"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The review describes exudation of fluid, plasma, proteins and leukocytes, with neutrophils named specifically. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What kind of organism is Helicobacter pylori, and what does it cause in the stomach?","choices":["A fungus that colonizes the gastric mucosa in immunosuppressed patients","A Gram-negative bacterium causing chronic gastritis","A Gram-positive bacterium that colonizes the stomach, causing acute ulceration and perforation","A virus that integrates its genome into gastric epithelial cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it colonizes the stomach and may lead to atrophic gastritis and intestinal metaplasia. It is the one bacterium among the microbial causes of cancer. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which structures detect linear acceleration and gravitational position?","choices":["The organ of Corti","The three semicircular canals","The otolith organs","The cupula and ampulla"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They handle static and linear balance, while the semicircular canals handle rotation. The two systems together give the brain a complete picture of head position and movement. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Why does cerumen impaction cause conductive rather than sensorineural loss?","choices":["It blocks the canal, so sound never reaches the conducting chain","It damages the outer hair cells","It stiffens the stapes footplate","It loads the tympanic membrane with fluid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The cochlea is untouched, and the defect is purely one of delivery. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which property distinguishes the monocyte from the neutrophil in terms of sustained phagocytic capacity?","choices":["The monocyte has a multilobed nucleus permitting repeated division","The monocyte can replace its lysosomal contents","The monocyte does not require opsonisation of its targets","The monocyte carries T cell receptors for direct antigen binding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The neutrophil cannot renew its lysosomes and dies after a few pathogens; the monocyte can replace its lysosomal contents and so keeps working. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A patient's inflamed tissue shows a leukocyte infiltrate dominated by cells with bi-lobed nuclei and pink-orange granules. Which process does this pattern most suggest?","choices":["A chronic autoimmune process","A humoral antibody response","An acute bacterial infection","An allergic or parasitic process"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bi-lobed nuclei with pink-orange granules identify eosinophils, which predominate in allergic reactions and parasitic infections. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of Meniere disease?","choices":["Defective endolymph resorption causing hydrops, ballooning the scala media until micro-ruptures occur","Inflammatory swelling and vascular congestion of the labyrinth after a viral infection","Dislodged otoconia floating in the semicircular canals","Demyelination of the vestibular nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Endolymphatic hydrops is the lesion: fluid accumulates because it is not resorbed, the membranous labyrinth distends, and micro-ruptures follow. The episodic nature follows from that cycle. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Why does a central chiasmal lesion spare nasal vision in both eyes?","choices":["The macula is spared by a dual blood supply","The lesion affects only one eye at a time","Only the crossing nasal retinal fibres are interrupted, and those carry the temporal fields; the temporal retinal fibres travel ipsilaterally and are untouched","The lesion damages the temporal retinal fibres instead, and those carry nasal vision"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the anatomy of the decussation explains which half is lost. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Where does each step of vitamin D activation take place, beginning in the skin?","choices":["Cholecalciferol is produced in the liver, then hydroxylated in the skin and finally in the kidneys","Cholecalciferol is produced in the skin and hydroxylated entirely within the kidneys in a single step","Cholecalciferol is produced in the skin under ultraviolet B light, then hydroxylated first in the liver and then in the kidneys to the active form calcitriol","Calcitriol is produced directly in the skin under ultraviolet B light and requires no further modification in either the liver or the kidneys at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The skin only starts the process; two further hydroxylation steps in separate organs are needed before the hormone is active. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which epidermal layer forms the semi-impermeable barrier, and what is it made of?","choices":["The stratum basale, a single layer of rapidly dividing columnar keratinocytes at the base","The stratum granulosum, 3 to 5 layers of diamond-shaped cells","The papillary dermis, a thin superficial connective tissue layer","The stratum corneum, 20 to 30 layers of keratin and dead keratinocytes cemented together"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That packing is what makes callused skin, and losing it is what makes barrier-defect conditions possible. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What causes petechiae, and what size defines them?","choices":["Minor bleeding into the dermis from capillary rupture, infection, thrombocytopenia or vasculitis, 1 to 2 millimetres or less","Permanent dilatation of the superficial dermal vessels, measuring 1 to 2 millimetres across the surface of the skin","Increased melanin production in the basal layer, up to 5 millimetres","Mast cell histamine release with plasma leakage, up to 5 millimetres"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and they are non-blanchable because the blood sits outside the vessel rather than within it. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which statement about neutrophils is correct?","choices":["They present antigen to T cells after phagocytosis","They are the predominant inflammatory cell in allergic reactions and in parasitic infection","They are first responders to microbial infection, and their activity and death in large numbers forms pus","They mature in the thymus and carry T cell receptors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both properties are given, and the second explains the composition of the suppurative pattern. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"In which cavity does seeding most commonly occur?","choices":["The subarachnoid space","The peritoneal cavity","The pericardial cavity","The pleural cavity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the pericardial and pleural cavities and the subarachnoid space are also named. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is a metastasis?","choices":["Local invasion of a cancer into the tissue immediately surrounding it","A benign nodule that develops in the tissue draining a cancer","A portion of a cancer that has migrated from the primary site to other sites","A second, unrelated primary cancer arising in another organ"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the primary remains where it began; the metastasis is what has left. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of the commonest, senile form of cataract?","choices":["Progressive insoluble aggregation and deposition of lens crystallin proteins","Osmotic swelling of the lens driven by sorbitol accumulating inside it","Rupture of the lens capsule after blunt trauma","Chronic ultraviolet-driven oxidative damage alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the molecular mechanism of the ageing cataract. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Why does asymmetrical vestibular input produce a false sense of motion?","choices":["The brain reads the mismatch between the two sides as movement","Cerebral perfusion falls on one side","The otoliths dissolve","The cochlea sends conflicting auditory signals"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Balance is a comparison, so unequal input is indistinguishable from turning. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is the mechanism and character of a chalazion?","choices":["An acute, tender, focal abscess of a gland sitting at the eyelid margin","A cholesterol-filled plaque along the nasal eyelid","Redundant lid skin with prolapse of orbital fat","A chronic, non-tender, sterile granulomatous lipogranuloma of a blocked meibomian gland"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — sterile and granulomatous is what separates it from an infection. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Why does prolonged antibiotic use predispose to FUNGAL otitis externa?","choices":["It thins the canal skin directly","It removes the bacterial competition that normally limits fungal growth","It raises the canal pH to favour fungi","It damages the tympanic membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The same logic as thrush: clear the residents and the fungus has the canal to itself. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the globe geometry in myopia, and where does the image focus?","choices":["The globe is normal but the lens has lost elasticity","The axial globe is too long, so the focal point falls in front of the retina","The axial globe is too short, so the focal point falls behind the retina","The globe is normal but the cornea is irregularly curved"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — an elongated globe overshoots, focusing short of the retina. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Who is the only one who can diagnose cancer?","choices":["The radiologist","The surgeon who obtains the specimen","The oncologist who stages the disease","The pathologist"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — he said it twice. Grading is a histological judgement made on tissue. — 2026-08-20 lecture recording, 22:36"},{"q":"What does it mean, structurally, for lymph nodes to be MATTED?","choices":["They have lost their individual capsules and move as one mass","They are tender and mobile","They are smaller than normal","They fluctuate in size with infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tissue no longer respecting its own boundaries is why matting raises concern. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Beyond phagocytosis, what is the monocyte's role in the immune response?","choices":["Secreting histamine to open the capillary bed","Producing the fibrous network that traps exudate","Presenting pieces of pathogens to T cells so the pathogen may be recognised again and killed","Restoring the immune system to its normal function once the infection has resolved"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This antigen-presenting role is what links the monocyte to later, faster responses against the same organism. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the bone remodelling sequence in otosclerosis?","choices":["Demineralisation of the ossicles from chronic inflammation","Fibrous scarring of the round window membrane","Osteoblastic overgrowth of dense cortical bone without resorption","Osteoclastic resorption, then spongy osteoid replacement"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bone is removed and replaced by soft vascular osteoid around the otic capsule and stapes footplate. The remodelled bone then fixes the footplate. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"How is the avascular cornea oxygenated?","choices":["By the choroidal circulation behind it","By direct contact with air and tears","By diffusion from the limbal blood vessels alone","By the aqueous humour circulating through the anterior chamber"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — air and tears oxygenate an avascular tissue. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What is metaplasia, and what provokes it?","choices":["Change of one cell type to another under chronic irritation or injury","Disordered growth of epithelial cells with variation in size and shape and loss of architectural orientation","Enlargement of an organ because its individual cells enlarge under increased demand","Autonomous growth of an abnormal mass of tissue derived from a single genetically altered cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it involves a change in differentiation. Bladder transitional epithelium becoming squamous is the standard example. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What produces lichenification, and what changes occur in the tissue?","choices":["Separation of the epidermis from the dermis with accumulation of fluid between the two layers, forming a large blister that lifts the surface away","Slowing of keratinocyte division with elastin degradation","Long-term scratching or rubbing of a pruritic area in an itch-scratch cycle, producing hyperplasia and hyperkeratosis with solid, firm, thick plaques without scaling","Loss of melanocytes across a circumscribed area"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and lichen simplex chronicus is the named condition. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What do initiators do in chemical carcinogenesis?","choices":["They cause permanent damage to deoxyribonucleic acid","They cause sustained or enhanced proliferation of already-damaged cells","They repair damage already caused by another carcinogen","They trigger apoptosis in cells with damaged genomes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — permanence is what distinguishes initiation from promotion. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Besides cervical cancer, which malignancies is high-risk human papillomavirus associated with?","choices":["Retinoblastoma and osteosarcoma","Anal, vulvar, vaginal, penile and oropharyngeal cancer","Hepatocellular carcinoma and gastric adenocarcinoma","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma and certain B cell lymphomas"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — all five are listed alongside cervical cancer, the oropharyngeal one being a squamous cell carcinoma. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is iris bombé?","choices":["Forward displacement of the iris against the cornea","Backward displacement of the iris against the lens","Atrophy of the iris stroma after an acute attack","Adhesion of the iris to the corneal endothelium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — forward bowing of the iris closes the angle. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What causes Beau's lines, and what are they associated with?","choices":["Impaired keratin synthesis, associated with deficiency anaemia","A halt of keratin production producing horizontal or transverse grooves, associated with illness, stress, injury to the nail and malnourishment","Overgrowth of connective tissue within the nail bed, associated with chronic liver disease and with diabetes mellitus in particular","Increased capillary density, associated with lung disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the groove marks the period during which the nail stopped being made. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"On the dysplasia spectrum, what separates severe dysplasia from invasive cancer?","choices":["The presence of mitoses, which appear only once invasion has occurred","Involvement of the regional lymph nodes","The basement membrane","The degree of nuclear enlargement, which is greater in invasive cancer"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — carcinoma in situ sits above it and invasive cancer has broken through. The diagram runs normal, mild, moderate, severe, carcinoma in situ, then invasive cancer. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What happens to the vitreous with age, and why does it matter for detachment?","choices":["It solidifies, pressing the retina more firmly against the pigment epithelium","It becomes vascularised, bleeding into the subretinal space","It thickens and opacifies, blocking light before it reaches the retina","It liquefies, so fluid can track through any retinal break into the subretinal space"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — Webster's jelly-in-the-fridge analogy, and the reason liquefaction is upstream of detachment. — Lecture recording, 26 August 2026"},{"q":"A lesion of the optic tract produces which defect?","choices":["Bitemporal hemianopsia","Monocular blindness","Central scotoma with preserved peripheral vision","Contralateral homonymous hemianopsia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — site C. Past the chiasm, each tract carries the same side of both eyes' fields. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"How are dermatofibroma and lipoma characterised in the tissue?","choices":["Both are malignant proliferations of keratinocytes","Dermatofibroma is keratin-filled and lipoma is fluid-filled","Dermatofibroma is a benign overgrowth of fibrous tissue in the dermis; lipoma is a benign enclosed capsule of adipocytes","Dermatofibroma is a benign enclosed capsule of adipocytes; lipoma is a fibrous tissue overgrowth within the dermis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both sit under the general term tumor, which describes rapid cellular growth and may or may not be malignant. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Histamine is described as which mediator in the inflammatory response, with what effects?","choices":["The final mediator released during the whole response, causing fibrosis and the formation of dense scar tissue at the site","The first mediator of the initial inflammatory response, causing dilation of arterioles and increased permeability of capillaries and venules","A chemotactic factor drawing neutrophils to the site","An opsonin that coats foreign material for recognition"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both the primacy and the two vascular effects are given. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Histamine and serotonin share which pair of effects?","choices":["Chemotaxis and opsonisation","Vasodilation and increased vascular permeability","Pain and fibrin deposition","Antibody production and antigen presentation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both are credited with dilating vessels and raising permeability, which is why they are the earliest vascular mediators. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which physical law explains why a small septal deviation causes disproportionate obstruction?","choices":["Boyle's law","Poiseuille's law","Fick's law of diffusion","Laplace's law"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Resistance rises with the fourth power of the radius, so a modest narrowing has an outsized effect on airflow. That is why a deviation that looks minor can produce persistent obstruction. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 22"}]};
